  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated in the sections reviewed. A text search of the enrolled bill finds no amendment to Election Code 63.0101 or to the in-person ID list (only 63.0011 residence-address references appear).\n(b_no_id_remedy) not stated (in-person). For mail ballots, see (c): an applicant who gives none of the numbers states so.\n(c_mail_ballot_id) (section) Sec. 5.02, amending 84.002(a)(1-a) (application)\n(quote) (1-a) the following information: (A) the number of the applicant's driver's license, election identification certificate, or personal identification card issued by the Department of Public Safety; (B) if the applicant has not been issued a number described by Paragraph (A), the last four digits of the applicant's social security number; or (C) a statement by the applicant that the applicant has not been issued a number described by Paragraph (A) or (B);\n(section) Sec. 5.02, new 84.002(b-1)\n(quote) A person may use the number of a driver's license, election identification certificate, or personal identification card that has expired for the purpose of fulfilling the requirement under Subsection (a)(1-a) if the license or identification is otherwise valid.\n(section) Sec. 5.03, amending 84.011(a)(3-a)\n(quote) (3-a) a space for entering the information required under Section 84.002(a)(1-a);\n(section) Sec. 5.07, new 86.001(f)\n(quote) If the information required under Section 84.002(a)(1-a) included on the application does not identify the same voter identified on the applicant's application for voter registration under Section 13.002(c)(8), the clerk shall reject the application.\n(section) Sec. 5.08, new 86.002(g)\n(quote) The carrier envelope must include a space that is hidden from view when the envelope is sealed for the voter to enter the following information: (1) the number of the voter's driver's license, election identification certificate, or personal identification card issued by the Department of Public Safety; (2) if the voter has not been issued a number described by Subdivision (1), the last four digits of the voter's social security number; or (3) a statement by the applicant that the applicant has not been issued a number described by Subdivision (1) or (2).\n(section) Sec. 5.13, amending 87.041(b)(8)\n(quote) the information required under Section 86.002(g) provided by the voter identifies the same voter identified on the voter's application for voter registration under Section 13.002(c)(8).\n(d_proof_of_citizenship) not stated in sections reviewed (task limited the read to points (a)-(e) in Secs. 5.02, 5.03, 5.07, 5.08, 5.13).\n(e_signature_or_records_verification) (section) Sec. 5.13, amending 87.041(b)(2)\n(quote) neither the voter's signature on the ballot application nor the signature on the carrier envelope certificate is determined to have been executed by a person other than the voter, unless signed by a witness;\n(section) Sec. 5.13, new 87.041(d-1)\n(quote) If a voter provides the information required under Section 86.002(g) and it identifies the same voter identified on the voter's application for voter registration under Section 13.002(c)(8), the signature on the ballot application and on the carrier envelope certificate shall be rebuttably presumed to be the signatures of the voter.\n(section) Sec. 5.13, amending 87.041(e)\n(quote) In making the determination under Subsection (b)(2), to determine whether the signatures are those of the voter, the board may also compare the signatures with any known signature [two or more signatures] of the voter [made within the preceding six years and] on file with the county clerk or voter registrar [to determine whether the signatures are those of the voter].\n(amendment_note) Amends the Texas Election Code. Square brackets mark deleted words; added words are not marked in the plain-text view.\n\nSponsors (as printed): Author (Senate): Hughes\nSource: Enrolled version (F), Texas Legislature Online, 87th 2nd Called Session (LegSess 872); curl with browser user agent. History page via WebFetch. The bill has ten articles (general provisions; registration; conduct and security; observers; mail voting; assistance; fraud; enforcement; ineligible voters; repealer/effective date); only points (a)-(e) were collected as instructed.\n\nAuthors and sponsors as printed (all): {\"name\":\"Author (Senate): Hughes\",\"version_filed\":\"Enrolled. House sponsors printed: 'Murr | Lozano | Clardy | White | Jetton' (Murr first-named).\",\"source_url\":\"https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=872&Bill=SB1\",\"note\":\"Page prints one Senate author, 'Hughes'.\"}",
